{
  "dom_id": "step-q125643",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/f4042d4eb43310480f3c75673befbf58067a89704d20b9a63f0f6c7272179f3e.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\u222e}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$",
  "worked_solution": "The circulation $\u0393$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\u0393={\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/fabbbb78d8fd4d327fe82d1d93d8e0fc15dd33e909266e0ef88de931be28b951.png)\n\nSo, ${\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement I is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/712564e3b0ad0e23d2d9097eb02c0857130bdaa6619a005738205130ab66ac3a.png)\n\nSo, ${\u222e}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
